+There is no database to migrate back — the post catalog and everything else
+the app reads live in the build itself — so a rollback here is just a
+redeploy of the previous commit's already-built output, not a rebuild from
+source and not a data restore.
+
+1. Find the last commit on `main` that was known good — the one right before
+   whatever change you're rolling back. Your hosting platform's deploy
+   history should show which commit is currently live; confirm it matches
+   the commit you're rolling back away from before you proceed.
+2. Redeploy that earlier commit's build through your hosting platform's
+   normal redeploy or promote action, the same path a push to `main` would
+   otherwise take. Use the existing build artifact for that commit rather
+   than rebuilding it, so you're not relying on `npm run build` producing an
+   identical result a second time.
+3. Watch the deploy finish and note the new deploy ID or timestamp your host
+   assigns it — you'll want it if you need to confirm later which rollback
+   this was.
+
+### Confirming it worked
+
+Check the following once the redeploy reports as finished:
+
+- The commit or build ID your hosting platform shows as currently live is
+  the one you rolled back to, not the one you rolled back from.
+- The home page loads and shows the portrait, bio, and post list.
+- A post with more than one translation still resolves through
+  `shared/resolve-translation.ts`'s fallback order: a browser sending an
+  `Accept-Language` header that matches a translation exactly gets that
+  translation, and a header matching nothing gets the post's default locale
+  instead of a blank page.
+- Nothing in your error tracking or host's request logs spikes in the
+  minutes right after the redeploy.
+
+If any of these don't hold, redeploying didn't fix the problem, or the
+rollback didn't actually take — treat that as a new incident rather than
+redeploying the same build again and hoping.
+
+### Who to tell
+
+Production deploys on every push to `main`, so `main` no longer matches
+what's live the moment you roll back. Tell anyone who has pushed to `main`,
+or has a pull request waiting to merge, since the last known-good commit —
+otherwise they'll assume their change shipped when it didn't. Say which
+commit is live now, what got rolled back, and why, in whatever place your
+team already tracks incidents or in the pull request that caused the
+rollback.
